{% doc %}
  The Tee of the Week collector slab — the sealed, graded, tilting card.

  @param {object} tee - The product the card describes. Everything printed on the
    card is read off it; nothing on the slab is typed in.
  @param {string} [series] - Set name, top-left of the grading label.
  @param {string} [subset] - Subset line, under the set name and on the plate.
  @param {string} [window_style] - photo | cutout | white.
  @param {object} [art] - Image override for the window. Falls back to the
    product's featured image.
  @param {string} [series_start] - Anchor date the week number counts from.
  @param {boolean} [standalone] - True when there is no purple band behind the
    slab already, i.e. everywhere except the homepage section. Paints the slab
    its own ground; see the note in aj-tee-slab.css.
  @param {boolean} [linked] - Whether the window links to the product. Off on the
    product page, where the link would point at the page you are already on.

  ONE IMPLEMENTATION, TWO PAGES. This was inline in `sections/aj-tee-week.liquid`
  until the product page needed the same card. Copying it would have meant every
  future change to the card landing on one page and not the other — and the
  divergence would only show up when somebody happened to look at both.

  THE DEFAULTS MATCH THE SECTION SCHEMA, so a caller that passes nothing gets the
  homepage card. If a default here and a default there ever disagree the product
  page silently drifts, which is exactly the seam this extraction was meant to
  close — they are listed together so the pairing is visible.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign v = tee.selected_or_first_available_variant

  assign off_pct = 0
  if v.compare_at_price > v.price
    assign off_pct = v.compare_at_price | minus: v.price | times: 100 | divided_by: v.compare_at_price
  endif

  # THE SERIES NUMBER IS DERIVED FROM THE CALENDAR, not a field somebody has to
  # remember to bump. A number that silently stops incrementing is worse than no
  # number, because it makes a weekly thing look abandoned. 604800 is a week in
  # seconds.
  assign anchor = series_start | default: '2026-08-04'
  assign anchor_s = anchor | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  assign now_s = 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  assign week_no = now_s | minus: anchor_s | divided_by: 604800 | plus: 1
  if week_no < 1
    assign week_no = 1
  endif

  # Zero-padded to three digits, applied cumulatively — testing `week_no` against
  # both bounds while re-deriving from the raw number each time would give single
  # digits only one zero.
  assign week_label = week_no | append: ''
  if week_no < 100
    assign week_label = week_label | prepend: '0'
  endif
  if week_no < 10
    assign week_label = week_label | prepend: '0'
  endif

  # Size range off whichever option is actually named size. Suppliers in this
  # catalogue are not consistent about option ORDER, so matching on the name is
  # the only reliable read.
  assign size_first = ''
  assign size_last = ''
  for opt in tee.options_with_values
    assign opt_name = opt.name | downcase
    if opt_name contains 'size'
      assign size_first = opt.values | first
      assign size_last = opt.values | last
    endif
  endfor

  # The slab serial. A real one, not a random string: the variant's SKU if the
  # supplier set one, otherwise the product id, which is stable and unique.
  assign serial = v.sku
  if serial == blank
    assign serial = tee.id
  endif

  assign set_name = series | default: 'The Yoink'
  assign subset_name = subset | default: 'Tee of the Week'
  assign window = window_style | default: 'photo'

  assign card_art = art
  if card_art == blank
    assign card_art = tee.featured_image
  endif

-%}

{%- if tee != blank -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    THE SNIPPET LOADS ITS OWN ASSETS so a caller cannot render the card and
    forget the CSS — which would leave a stack of unstyled text where a sealed
    card should be, and only on whichever page was added last. Two pages use this
    now; the third will not have to remember anything.

    The script is progressive enhancement, never a dependency: the slab leans,
    lifts, straightens and catches the light in pure CSS. What the file adds is
    cursor tracking, which is the difference between a card that reacts and a
    card that feels held.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {{ 'aj-tee-slab.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  <script src="{{ 'aj-tee-slab.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>

  {%- comment -%}
    THE WRAPPER IS CONDITIONAL, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy. On the
    homepage `.aj-teew__stage` is a direct grid child of `.aj-teew__inner` and the
    desktop layout puts it in the left column; wrapping it unconditionally would
    have made the wrapper the grid item and collapsed that layout. Emitting it
    only for standalone callers leaves the homepage DOM exactly as it was.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if standalone -%}<div class="aj-teeslab aj-teeslab--standalone">{%- endif -%}
    <div class="aj-teew__stage">
      <div class="aj-teew__slab" data-aj-tilt>
        {%- comment -%}
          The case itself: one element for the plastic, one for the light running
          across it. Both inert.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span class="aj-teew__glass" aria-hidden="true"></span>

        {%- comment -%}
          The grading label. Set name on the left, week number where a grade
          would sit, serial and barcode beneath — the barcode is drawn with a
          repeating gradient, so it encodes nothing and claims nothing.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="aj-teew__label">
          <div class="aj-teew__label-l">
            <span class="aj-teew__label-set">{{ set_name | escape }}</span>
            <span class="aj-teew__label-sub">{{ subset_name | escape }}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="aj-teew__label-r">
            <span class="aj-teew__label-wk">Wk</span>
            <span class="aj-teew__label-no">{{ week_label }}</span>
          </div>
          <span class="aj-teew__barcode" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="aj-teew__serial">{{ serial | escape }}</span>
        </div>

        <article class="aj-teew__card">
          <span class="aj-teew__bands" aria-hidden="true"></span>

          <div class="aj-teew__cardin">
            <span class="aj-teew__roundel" aria-hidden="true">Y</span>

            {%- comment -%}
              The window is decorative in both cases — the card repeats a title
              and a price that are already on the page, so it is hidden from
              assistive technology rather than read out twice.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              LAZY ON THE HOMEPAGE, EAGER STANDALONE. The section sits well down
              the page, so deferring the art is free. On the product page the card
              IS the hero — it is the first thing above the fold — and lazy left
              an empty cream window for the first second of every visit.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- liquid
              assign art_loading = 'lazy'
              assign art_priority = 'auto'
              if standalone
                assign art_loading = 'eager'
                assign art_priority = 'high'
              endif
            -%}
            {%- capture card_window -%}
              {%- if card_art != blank -%}
                {{
                  card_art
                  | image_url: width: 900
                  | image_tag:
                    loading: art_loading,
                    fetchpriority: art_priority,
                    widths: '360, 540, 720, 900',
                    sizes: '(min-width: 750px) 32vw, 72vw',
                    class: 'aj-teew__art',
                    alt: ''
                }}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endcapture -%}

            {%- if linked == false -%}
              <span class="aj-teew__window aj-teew__window--{{ window }}" aria-hidden="true">
                {{- card_window -}}
              </span>
            {%- else -%}
              <a
                class="aj-teew__window aj-teew__window--{{ window }}"
                href="{{ tee.url }}"
                tabindex="-1"
                aria-hidden="true"
              >
                {{- card_window -}}
              </a>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              The name plate, which on a real card is the only place the subject
              is named — so the product title goes here and nowhere else on the
              slab.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <div class="aj-teew__plate">
              <h3 class="aj-teew__name">{{ tee.title | escape }}</h3>
              <p class="aj-teew__plate-sub">
                <span aria-hidden="true">&#9733;</span>
                {{ subset_name | escape }}
                <span aria-hidden="true">&#9733;</span>
              </p>
            </div>

            {%- comment -%}
              Every figure here is read off the product. Nothing in this strip is
              typed into the editor, so it cannot drift away from the thing it
              describes.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <dl class="aj-teew__strip">
              <div class="aj-teew__stat">
                <dt>Price</dt>
                <dd class="aj-teew__stat-price">{{ v.price | money }}</dd>
              </div>
              {%- if off_pct > 0 -%}
                <div class="aj-teew__stat">
                  <dt>Was</dt>
                  <dd><s>{{ v.compare_at_price | money }}</s></dd>
                </div>
              {%- endif -%}
              {%- if size_first != blank -%}
                <div class="aj-teew__stat">
                  <dt>Sizes</dt>
                  <dd>
                    {{- size_first | escape -}}
                    {%- if size_last != size_first %}&ndash;{{ size_last | escape }}{% endif -%}
                  </dd>
                </div>
              {%- endif -%}
            </dl>

            <span class="aj-teew__sheen" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </div>
        </article>

        {%- comment -%}
          THE SAVING, AT THE SIZE IT DESERVES. It was a small corner sticker while
          the concern was that 15% would be read against the Bargain Bin's 25%. On
          the page that fear was misplaced — the card frames it as a perk on a
          curated pick rather than as a competing deal, so the number can be loud
          without inviting the comparison.

          Still computed, never typed: this whole block is inside `off_pct > 0`
          and prints whatever the real gap is.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if off_pct > 0 -%}
          <span class="aj-teew__flag">
            <span class="aj-teew__flag-n">{{ off_pct }}%</span>
            <span class="aj-teew__flag-t">off</span>
          </span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>
  {%- if standalone -%}</div>{%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
